<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<section xml:id="sec-6-3-structure">
  <title>Structure of an Object-Oriented Solution</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Our programs moving forward will be composed of at least two files that you will 
      place in the same Eclipse project:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          A program class like what you have been writing up to now, with a <c>main</c> 
          method where program execution begins;
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          One or more class files, defining types that the <c>main</c> method can use.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The class definitions are new data types that model entities within the problem 
      domain. The <c>main</c> method will create instances (objects) of those classes and 
      invoke methods on them to fulfill the system's responsibilities. As we learn about 
      objects and classes in this chapter, our <c>main</c> method will be a procedurally 
      designed program that uses objects to fulfill system responsibilities. In a larger-scale 
      object-oriented system, control is more distributed: many objects collaborate to fulfill 
      system responsibilities without being organized into a rigid hierarchical structure.
    </p>

    <p>
      We will use a small example to illustrate class design and object creation.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-3-1-class-definition">
    <title>A Class Definition</title>

    <p>
      <xref ref="listing-point-class"/> is a bare-bones class definition for a class 
      called <c>Point</c>. There are no comments in this class so you can focus on the 
      code differences between this type definition and the programs we have written 
      previously. See what you recognize and what looks different from our prior examples:
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing-point-class">
      <caption>The <c>Point</c> class definition</caption>
      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
        <code>
public class Point {
    
    private double x;
    private double y;
    
    public Point(double x, double y) {
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
    }
    
    public double distanceFromOrigin() {
        return Math.sqrt((this.x * this.x) + (this.y * this.y));
    }
    
    public void translate(double dx, double dy) {
        this.x += dx;
        this.y += dy;
    }
    
    public double getX() {
        return this.x;
    }
    
    public double getY() {
        return this.y;
    }
}
        </code>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      What differences do you observe from our prior programs? You probably noticed:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>There is no <c>main</c> method!</strong> If you run this class as a 
          Java program, a <c>.class</c> file will be created but nothing else will happen. 
          <c>Point</c> is a type that can be used in other classes.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          There are variable declarations at the top of the class that begin with a new 
          keyword: <c>private</c>. These are the <em>fields</em> of a <c>Point</c> object. 
          The <c>private</c> keyword limits the visibility of the fields to the methods 
          within the class.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          There is a method with the same name as the class and no return type (<c>Point</c>). 
          This is a special type of method called a <em>constructor</em>. It is called when 
          an object of class <c>Point</c> is created with the <c>new</c> keyword (from a 
          method in another class), and it gives initial values to all of the object's fields.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Inside each method, references to the fields are prepended with a new keyword: 
          <c>this</c>. The <c>this</c> keyword refers to the object on which the method was 
          invoked. When a parameter variable has the exact same name as a field (as in our 
          constructor), the parameter <em>shadows</em> (hides) the field. Prepending the 
          field with <c>this.</c> (<q>this dot</q>) tells Java to assign the parameter 
          <c>x</c> to the field <c>x</c> belonging to this specific object.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      We will take time throughout this chapter to explore each of these new concepts in 
      detail. For now, try to get used to the overall structure of a class. It will help to 
      see <c>Point</c> being used by a class with a <c>main</c> method.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-3-2-main-using-point">
    <title>A <c>main</c> Method That Uses <c>Point</c></title>

    <p>
      <xref ref="listing-point-demo"/> is an interactive program called <c>PointDemo</c> 
      that creates <c>Point</c> objects and invokes methods on them.
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing-point-demo">
      <caption>Class with a <c>main</c> method that uses <c>Point</c></caption>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="java">
        <code>
public class PointDemo {
    
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Declares and instantiates two separate Point objects.
        Point p1 = new Point(3.0, 4.0);
        Point p2 = new Point(5.0, 12.0);
    
        // Asks each object to calculate its own distance to (0, 0).
        System.out.println("Point 1 coordinates: (" + p1.getX() + ", " + p1.getY() + ")");
        System.out.println("Point 1 distance to origin: " + p1.distanceFromOrigin()); 
    
        System.out.println("\nPoint 2 coordinates: (" + p2.getX() + ", " 
                           + p2.getY() + ")");
        System.out.println("Point 2 distance to origin: " + p2.distanceFromOrigin()); 
    
        // Moves p1 and recalculates.
        p1.translate(3.0, 4.0); 
        System.out.println("\nAfter moving p1 to (" + p1.getX() + ", " 
                           + p1.getY() + "):");
        System.out.println("New distance to origin: " + p1.distanceFromOrigin()); 
    }
}
        </code>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      This is the output of <c>PointDemo</c>. See if you can trace how the program produces 
      this output before you read on:
    </p>

    <console>
      <output>
Point 1 coordinates: (3.0, 4.0)
Point 1 distance to origin: 5.0

Point 2 coordinates: (5.0, 12.0)
Point 2 distance to origin: 13.0

After moving p1 to (6.0, 8.0):
New distance to origin: 10.0
      </output>
    </console>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-3-3-how-they-work-together">
    <title>How They Work Together</title>

    <p>
      Let's walk through the execution of <c>PointDemo</c>, beginning with lines 5 and 6<mdash/>object 
      construction:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          On line 5, we declare a variable, <c>p1</c>, of type <c>Point</c>. In the same line, 
          we create an instance of <c>Point</c> (an object) using the <c>new</c> keyword. 
          We pass two arguments to the constructor: <c>3.0</c> and <c>4.0</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          In response, Java allocates memory for the object in the heap (enough space to 
          store the fields, <c>x</c> and <c>y</c>), and control jumps to the constructor 
          (<c>Point</c>), with arguments <c>3.0</c> and <c>4.0</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The constructor in <c>Point</c> begins execution. <c>this.x</c> is assigned the value 
          of the parameter variable <c>x</c>, which holds <c>3.0</c> (copied from the argument). 
          What is <c>this</c>? It is a reference to the new object on the heap. When we 
          assign <c>3.0</c> to <c>this.x</c>, we are assigning a value to the <c>x</c> field 
          of the new <c>Point</c> object. We then assign the value in the parameter variable 
          <c>y</c> to <c>this.y</c>, the constructor finishes executing, and control returns 
          to line 5 in <c>PointDemo.main</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Line 5 is an assignment statement. The right side has finished executing, so Java 
          places a reference to the new <c>Point</c> object into the variable <c>p1</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Line 6 executes. This is very similar to line 5<mdash/>we are creating another 
          <c>Point</c> object, but this time with different values for <c>x</c> and <c>y</c>. 
          Java allocates another block of memory on the heap for a second <c>Point</c> object.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The constructor executes again, but this time, <c>this</c> is a reference to the 
          new <c>Point</c> object in the heap that was just allocated. Its fields receive 
          the values <c>5.0</c> and <c>12.0</c>. The constructor finishes executing and control 
          returns to <c>PointDemo.main</c>, where the assignment statement completes and Java 
          stores the reference to the new object in <c>p2</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      There are now two <c>Point</c> objects in the heap: one is referenced by <c>p1</c> 
      and holds <c>3.0</c> in its <c>x</c> field and <c>4.0</c> in its <c>y</c> field; the 
      second is referenced by <c>p2</c> and holds <c>5.0</c> in its <c>x</c> field and <c>12.0</c> 
      in its <c>y</c> field. <xref ref="fig-point-demo-initial"/> depicts the stack and the 
      heap at this point during execution.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-point-demo-initial">
      <caption>The stack and heap after line 6 in <c>PointDemo.main</c> executes.</caption>
      <image source="figures/figure_6_1.png" width="95%">
        <description>
          A memory diagram showing the call stack on the left and the heap on the right. 
          On the call stack, the main method frame contains two reference variables: p1 holding 
          address 0x100, and p2 holding address 0x200. On the heap, two separate Point objects 
          are allocated. The first object at address 0x100 contains field x equal to 3.0 and field 
          y equal to 4.0, with an arrow pointing from p1 to address 0x100. The second object at 
          address 0x200 contains field x equal to 5.0 and field y equal to 12.0, with an arrow 
          pointing from p2 to address 0x200.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      These objects will stay in the heap until there are no references to them. The references 
      to <c>p1</c> and <c>p2</c> have a lifetime that extends to the end of <c>main</c>, so 
      the data will remain in the heap until <c>main</c> finishes executing.
    </p>

    <p>
      Execution now reaches line 9 of <c>main</c>, and the program begins to invoke methods 
      on the two objects. On lines 9<ndash/>14, accessor methods are invoked. <em>Accessor methods</em> 
      are value-returning methods that access the internal state of an object without changing 
      the object. They sometimes return the values stored in fields (like <c>getX</c> and 
      <c>getY</c>), and they sometimes use the values in fields to answer more complicated 
      questions about the object (like <c>distanceFromOrigin</c>).
    </p>

    <p>
      Let's continue with our step-by-step walkthrough of <c>main</c> and focus on the accessors:
    </p>

    <ol start="7">
      <li>
        <p>
          In <c>PointDemo.main</c>, line 9 executes. On this line, we invoke two methods, one 
          after the other, on <c>p1</c>. First, we invoke the <c>getX</c> method. Execution 
          jumps to <c>getX</c> in <c>Point</c>, which returns the value in <c>this.x</c>. 
          When we invoke the method on <c>p1</c>, the reference from <c>p1</c> is copied into 
          <c>this</c>. When <c>getX</c> returns <c>this.x</c>, it returns <c>3.0</c><mdash/>the 
          value stored in <c>x</c> in the <c>Point</c> object in the heap referenced by <c>p1</c>. 
          Back in <c>PointDemo.main</c>, we then invoke <c>getY</c>, execution jumps to <c>getY</c> 
          in <c>Point</c>, and the method returns <c>p1</c>'s <c>y</c> value: <c>4.0</c>. The 
          coordinates are printed, giving us our first line of output.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Back in <c>PointDemo.main</c>, line 10 executes. This line invokes 
          <c>distanceFromOrigin</c> on <c>p1</c>. Execution jumps to <c>distanceFromOrigin</c> 
          in <c>Point</c>, which returns the result of the distance formula, calculating the 
          distance from <m>(0, 0)</m> to <m>(\text{this.x}, \text{this.y})</m>. Because the method 
          is invoked on <c>p1</c>, <c>this.x</c> is <c>3.0</c> and <c>this.y</c> is <c>4.0</c>. 
          The method returns <c>5.0</c> to line 10 in <c>PointDemo.main</c>, which prints it.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Execution moves to line 12, which is equivalent to line 9, except the methods 
          <c>getX</c> and <c>getY</c> are invoked on <c>p2</c>. Therefore it is <c>p2</c>'s 
          reference that is copied into <c>this</c>. When <c>this.x</c> is returned in <c>Point</c>, 
          <c>5.0</c> is returned; when <c>this.y</c> is returned, <c>12.0</c> is returned. 
          The values are printed.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Similarly, when line 14 executes in <c>PointDemo.main</c>, <c>distanceFromOrigin</c> 
          is invoked on <c>p2</c>, so the distance from <m>(0, 0)</m> to <m>(5.0, 12.0)</m> is 
          calculated, returned, and printed.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      When we invoke a method on an object, the code for that method in the class definition 
      executes. The data that it executes on<mdash/>the object's fields<mdash/>are the fields of the 
      object before the dot in the invocation. When we invoke a method on <c>p1</c>, such as 
      <c>p1.distanceFromOrigin()</c>, <c>this.x</c> and <c>this.y</c> are the <c>x</c> and 
      <c>y</c> stored in the <c>Point</c> in the heap that <c>p1</c> references. When we invoke 
      the same method on <c>p2</c>, <c>this.x</c> and <c>this.y</c> are the variables in the 
      <c>Point</c> in the heap that <c>p2</c> references. Methods are the way that we access 
      or change an object's state.
    </p>

    <p>
      Methods that change an object's state are called <em>mutators</em>. The <c>Point</c> class 
      has one mutator: <c>translate</c>. It takes two parameters: <c>dx</c> and <c>dy</c>. 
      These are the values by which we shift <c>this.x</c> and <c>this.y</c>. We invoke the 
      <c>translate</c> method once in <c>PointDemo</c>:
    </p>

    <ol start="11">
      <li>
        <p>
          When execution reaches line 17, the <c>translate</c> method is invoked on <c>p1</c>, 
          with arguments <c>3.0</c> and <c>4.0</c>. Execution jumps to <c>translate</c> in 
          <c>Point</c>. <c>this.x</c> and <c>this.y</c> are on the left side of an assignment 
          statement, so they refer to the memory locations on the heap that <c>p1</c> references. 
          When <c>3.0</c> (<c>dx</c>) is added to <c>this.x</c>, the result is <c>6.0</c>, stored 
          in <c>this.x</c> in the heap. When <c>4.0</c> (<c>dy</c>) is added to <c>this.y</c>, 
          the result is <c>8.0</c>, stored in <c>this.y</c> in the heap.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Execution proceeds to line 18, which invokes <c>getX</c> and <c>getY</c> on <c>p1</c>. 
          Because <c>p1</c> has new values, the updated coordinates <m>(6.0, 8.0)</m> are printed.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Execution proceeds to line 20, which invokes <c>distanceFromOrigin</c> on <c>p1</c>. 
          Again, because <c>p1</c>'s state has changed, a new distance from the origin (<c>10.0</c>) 
          is returned and printed.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      <xref ref="fig-point-demo-translated"/> depicts the stack and the heap after line 17 in 
      <c>PointDemo.main</c> executes.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-point-demo-translated">
      <caption>The stack and heap after line 17 in <c>PointDemo.main</c> executes.</caption>
      <image source="figures/figure_6_2.png" width="95%">
        <description>
          A memory diagram showing the call stack on the left and the heap on the right after 
          p1 is translated. On the call stack, p1 still references address 0x100 and p2 still 
          references address 0x200. On the heap, the Point object at 0x100 now contains updated 
          fields x equal to 6.0 and y equal to 8.0. The second Point object at 0x200 remains 
          unchanged with x equal to 5.0 and y equal to 12.0.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </subsection>
</section>